            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Alexander Fleming's Early Life</strong><br>\nAlexander Fleming was born on August 6, 1881, in Ayrshire, Scotland. He grew up in a rural area and later moved to London, where he attended the St. Mary's Hospital Medical School. Fleming had a keen interest in the natural world from a young age, which led him to study medicine. His work at St. Mary's Hospital laid the foundation for his later discoveries in bacteriology, the study of bacteria.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War I Experience</strong><br>\nDuring World War I, Fleming served in the Royal Army Medical Corps. He witnessed many soldiers dying from sepsis, a severe infection caused by bacteria, which antibiotics could not yet treat. This experience deeply affected him and motivated his future work. Fleming's observations of antiseptics failing to prevent infections in deep wounds led him to search for more effective substances.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Discovering Lysozyme</strong><br>\nIn 1921, Alexander Fleming discovered lysozyme, an enzyme with mild antibacterial properties found in bodily fluids like tears and saliva. This discovery happened by accident when he noticed that nasal mucus had fallen onto a plate of bacteria, causing the bacteria to dissolve. Although lysozyme was not powerful enough to treat major infections, it was an important step toward discovering penicillin.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
