      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Accidental Discovery of Penicillin</strong><br>\nFleming's most famous discovery, penicillin, occurred in 1928 when he observed that a mold called Penicillium notatum had contaminated a petri dish of Staphylococcus bacteria and had killed many of the bacteria. This accidental discovery led to the development of penicillin, the first true antibiotic, which could kill a wide range of bacteria without harming the human body.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Impact of Penicillin</strong><br>\nPenicillin revolutionized medicine by providing an effective treatment for bacterial infections, such as sepsis, pneumonia, and gonorrhea, which were often fatal before its use. Its widespread use began in the 1940s after further development by scientists Howard Florey and Ernst Boris Chain, saving countless lives during World War II and beyond.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Recognition</strong><br>\nEven after discovering penicillin, Fleming faced challenges in purifying and producing the drug in large quantities. It took over a decade before penicillin could be mass-produced. In 1945, Fleming received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine along with Florey and Chain for this life-saving discovery.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
