          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Fleming's Later Research</strong><br>\nAfter his monumental discovery, Fleming continued to conduct research at St. Mary's Hospital. He investigated possible uses of penicillin and worked on improving antibiotics. Fleming also warned early on about the potential for bacteria to develop resistance to antibiotics if they were overused or used improperly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nFleming was known for being modest about his achievements, often attributing his discoveries to good fortune. He was knighted in 1944 and became known as Sir Alexander Fleming. His discovery of penicillin has saved millions of lives, making him one of the most celebrated scientists in history.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Lasting Influence</strong><br>\nAlexander Fleming died on March 11, 1955, in London. The legacy of his work continues to influence modern medicine. The discovery of penicillin began the era of antibiotics, which has transformed medical treatment and led to the development of many more antibiotics, changing the course of healthcare forever.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
